"executive_summary": "American Foulbrood (AFB), caused by Paenibacillus larvae, is a devastating honeybee disease often missed due to subjective visual diagnosis and laborious sampling methods requiring hive dismantling. This study introduces the 'Foster method,' a rapid, non-invasive environmental DNA (eDNA) sampling technique using hive entrance swabs combined with a dual-target qPCR assay (AFBduo) to detect clinically significant AFB levels. The AFBduo assay targets the single-copy ftsZ gene and the multi-copy 16S gene, offering high sensitivity (down to 4 copies) and specificity. The study evaluated P. larvae levels in nurse bees, whole baseboard swabs, and entrance swabs across three cohorts of hives in New Zealand quarantine apiaries. Results demonstrated that entrance swab spore levels correlated strongly with clinical disease status. A spore quantity of 565,000 or more indicated a 95% probability of clinical signs. Notably, the method detected pre-clinical infections in hives without visible symptoms, allowing for early intervention. The study also validated a pooling strategy for swab eluates to reduce costs and time. The Foster method eliminates the need to dismantle hives, offering a scalable solution for AFB surveillance and potential application to other bee pathogens and incursion pests.",
